  {
   "id": "ZNex48ynjMkJ",
   "from": "D Holmes",
   "author": "e3fc8587db796618",
   "date": "2015-03-28T14:54:39Z",
   "subject": "Re: Expanding each row across a range",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Expensive, but there&#39;s always outer joins on a common variable.<br><br><span style=\"border-collapse:separate;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:11px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:12px;text-align:-webkit-left;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:pre-wrap;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(225,226,229)\"><pre style=\"font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:8pt!important;outline-style:none;outline-width:initial;outline-color:initial;border-top-style:none;border-right-style:none;border-bottom-style:none;border-left-style:none;border-width:initial;border-color:initial;white-space:pre-wrap!important;word-break:break-all;margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;line-height:1.2\"></pre></span></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><span style=\"border-collapse:separate;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:11px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:12px;text-align:-webkit-left;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:pre-wrap;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(225,226,229)\"><pre style=\"font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:8pt!important;outline-style:none;outline-width:initial;outline-color:initial;border-top-style:none;border-right-style:none;border-bottom-style:none;border-left-style:none;border-width:initial;border-color:initial;white-space:pre-wrap!important;word-break:break-all;margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;line-height:1.2\"><span style=\"white-space:pre;color:rgb(127,0,85)\">&gt; </span><span style=\"color:rgb(127,0,85)\">df &lt;- data.frame(x=c(&quot;a&quot;, &quot;b&quot;, &quot;c&quot;), start=1:3, end=4:6, val=c(100, 200, 300))<br></span></pre></span></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><span style=\"border-collapse:separate;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:11px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:12px;text-align:-webkit-left;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:pre-wrap;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(225,226,229)\"><pre style=\"font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:8pt!important;outline-style:none;outline-width:initial;outline-color:initial;border-top-style:none;border-right-style:none;border-bottom-style:none;border-left-style:none;border-width:initial;border-color:initial;white-space:pre-wrap!important;word-break:break-all;margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;line-height:1.2\"><span style=\"white-space:pre;color:rgb(127,0,85)\">&gt; </span><span style=\"color:rgb(127,0,85)\">test1=plyr::ddply(df, .(x), function (row) data.frame(n=row$start:row$end, val=row$val/length(row$start:row$end)))\r\n</span><span style=\"white-space:pre;color:rgb(127,0,85)\">&gt; </span><span style=\"color:rgb(127,0,85)\">test2=full_join(mutate(df,com=1),data.frame(n=1:max(df$end),com=1)) %&gt;% filter(n&gt;=start &amp; n&lt;=end) %&gt;% transmute(x,n,val=val/(end-start+1))\r\n</span><span>Joining by: &quot;com&quot;\r\n</span><span style=\"white-space:pre;color:rgb(127,0,85)\">&gt; </span><span style=\"color:rgb(127,0,85)\">identical(test1,test2)</span></pre></span><span style=\"border-collapse:separate;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:11px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:12px;text-align:-webkit-left;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:pre-wrap;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(225,226,229)\"><pre style=\"font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:8pt!important;outline-style:none;outline-width:initial;outline-color:initial;border-top-style:none;border-right-style:none;border-bottom-style:none;border-left-style:none;border-width:initial;border-color:initial;white-space:pre-wrap!important;word-break:break-all;margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;line-height:1.2\">[1] TRUE</pre></span><br>Interesting that &quot;<span style=\"color:rgb(127,0,85)\">filter(between(n,start,end)) </span><span style=\"border-collapse:separate;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:11px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:12px;text-align:-webkit-left;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:pre-wrap;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(225,226,229)\"></span>&quot; didn&#39;t work -- it seems to want a scalar for the start and end arguments.  This might be a small enhancement for future dplyr.</div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><br><br>On Saturday, March 28, 2015 at 12:41:57 AM UTC-5, Andrew Joyner wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">I have row data that includes a range start and end, and I want to distribute a numeric value uniformly across the range by creating additional rows, one for each element in the range.<div><br></div><div>For example, the following approach gives me what I need...<div><br></div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">df </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">frame</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;a&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;b&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;c&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> start</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">end</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#066\">6</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> val</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><font color=\"#006666\"><span style=\"color:#000\">c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">100</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">200</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">300</span></font><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>plyr</span><span style=\"color:#660\">::</span><span style=\"color:#000\">ddply</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">df</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">.(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">function</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><font color=\"#000000\"><span style=\"color:#000\">row</span></font><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">frame</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">row$start</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">row$end</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> val</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">row$val</span><span style=\"color:#660\">/</span><span style=\"color:#000\">length</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">row$start</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">row</span><span style=\"color:#000\">$end</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)))</span></div></code></div><div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\"><br></font></div></div><div>The function creates a new data frame for each row and ddply bolts them all together. It seems ok, but is there a more natural way to do this with <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">tidyr </font>and <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">dplyr </font>that removes the $ syntax and the anonymous function?<br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div></div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "GZdvx7GyUxEJ",
   "from": "Dennis Murphy",
   "author": "0c39e60ddb38622b",
   "date": "2015-03-28T14:57:20Z",
   "subject": "Re: Expanding each row across a range",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "Hi:\r<br>\r<br>The rowwise() function can replace group_by() in this problem. The\r<br>following works for me:\r<br>\r<br>library(dplyr)\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "df &lt;- data.frame(x=c(&quot;a&quot;, &quot;b&quot;, &quot;c&quot;), start=1:3, end=4:6, val=c(100, 200, 300))\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "df %&gt;%\r<br>   rowwise() %&gt;%\r<br>   do(with(., data.frame(x = x, n = seq(start, end), val = val)))\r<br>\r<br>Dennis\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "\r<br>On Fri, Mar 27, 2015 at 10:41 PM, Andrew Joyner &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">and...@alphajuliet.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt; I have row data that includes a range start and end, and I want to\r<br>&gt; distribute a numeric value uniformly across the range by creating additional\r<br>&gt; rows, one for each element in the range.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; For example, the following approach gives me what I need...\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; df &lt;- data.frame(x=c(&quot;a&quot;, &quot;b&quot;, &quot;c&quot;), start=1:3, end=4:6, val=c(100, 200,\r<br>&gt; 300))\r<br>&gt; plyr::ddply(df, .(x), function (row) data.frame(n=row$start:row$end,\r<br>&gt; val=row$val/length(row$start:row$end)))\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; The function creates a new data frame for each row and ddply bolts them all\r<br>&gt; together. It seems ok, but is there a more natural way to do this with tidyr\r<br>&gt; and dplyr that removes the $ syntax and the anonymous function?\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "&gt; --\r<br>&gt; You received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups\r<br>&gt; &quot;manipulatr&quot; group.\r<br>&gt; To unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an\r<br>&gt; email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">manipulatr+...@googlegroups.com</a>.\r<br>&gt; To post to this group, send email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">manip...@googlegroups.com</a>.\r<br>&gt; Visit this group at <a href=\"http://groups.google.com/group/manipulatr\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://groups.google.com/group/manipulatr&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792357666316000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0nm7b0mHA-TsiPpFRak21N\">http://groups.google.com/group/manipulatr</a>.\r<br>&gt; For more options, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/optout\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/optout&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792357666316000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2mWHhyYywgrGApyzi3O-vt\">https://groups.google.com/d/optout</a>.\r<br>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "f0ADXzk2KG8J",
   "from": "Andrew Joyner",
   "author": "2fdd938b58361fa0",
   "date": "2015-03-28T23:00:39Z",
   "subject": "Re: Expanding each row across a range",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">I&#39;m liking this answer. Thanks for the reminder about <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">rowwise()</font>. As its help entry says: &quot;<span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:13.3333330154419px\"><i>It is also useful to support arbitrary complex operations that need to be applied to each row.</i>&quot; Sounds right.</span><div><br></div><div>In reality, the problem I&#39;m solving is about dividing efforts into bins across time intervals, and then summing the efforts in each bin, e.g. project resource demand by month. This has definitely helped to optimise my local issue and learn some more, but I suspect there&#39;s a better overall solution that I&#39;ll keep iterating towards. Thanks, all.</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>On Sunday, 29 March 2015 01:57:20 UTC+11, Dennis Murphy  wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">Hi:\r\n<br>\r\n<br>The rowwise() function can replace group_by() in this problem. The\r\n<br>following works for me:\r\n<br>\r\n<br>library(dplyr)\r\n<br>df &lt;- data.frame(x=c(&quot;a&quot;, &quot;b&quot;, &quot;c&quot;), start=1:3, end=4:6, val=c(100, 200, 300))\r\n<br>df %&gt;%\r\n<br>   rowwise() %&gt;%\r\n<br>   do(with(., data.frame(x = x, n = seq(start, end), val = val)))\r\n<br>\r\n<br>Dennis\r\n<br>\r\n<br>On Fri, Mar 27, 2015 at 10:41 PM, Andrew Joyner &lt;<a rel=\"nofollow\">and...@alphajuliet.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r\n<br>&gt; I have row data that includes a range start and end, and I want to\r\n<br>&gt; distribute a numeric value uniformly across the range by creating additional\r\n<br>&gt; rows, one for each element in the range.\r\n<br>&gt;\r\n<br>&gt; For example, the following approach gives me what I need...\r\n<br>&gt;\r\n<br>&gt; df &lt;- data.frame(x=c(&quot;a&quot;, &quot;b&quot;, &quot;c&quot;), start=1:3, end=4:6, val=c(100, 200,\r\n<br>&gt; 300))\r\n<br>&gt; plyr::ddply(df, .(x), function (row) data.frame(n=row$start:row$end,\r\n<br>&gt; val=row$val/length(row$start:row$end)))\r\n<br>&gt;\r\n<br>&gt; The function creates a new data frame for each row and ddply bolts them all\r\n<br>&gt; together. It seems ok, but is there a more natural way to do this with tidyr\r\n<br>&gt; and dplyr that removes the $ syntax and the anonymous function?\r\n<br>&gt;\r\n<br>&gt;\r\n<br>&gt; --\r\n<br>&gt; You received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups\r\n<br>&gt; &quot;manipulatr&quot; group.\r\n<br>&gt; To unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an\r\n<br>&gt; email to <a rel=\"nofollow\">manipulatr+...@googlegroups.com</a>.\r\n<br>&gt; To post to this group, send email to <a rel=\"nofollow\">manip...@googlegroups.com</a>.\r\n<br>&gt; Visit this group at <a href=\"http://groups.google.com/group/manipulatr\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://groups.google.com/group/manipulatr&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792357666317000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0dOT2_nxCX_t5sZ1cER4CR\">http://groups.google.com/group/manipulatr</a>.\r\n<br>&gt; For more options, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/optout\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/optout&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792357666317000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3HL5QjufFCga98D2rWwMME\">https://groups.google.com/d/optout</a>.\r\n<br></blockquote></div></div>"
